WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.099
Good morning, Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.099 --> 00:00:03.669
I know that blank wall bothers you, I just
can't think of what to do with it.

00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:05.459
If I move the camera over here, there's too
much light.

00:00:05.459 --> 00:00:07.540
I guess I could just zoom in, but then my
face looks weird.

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:10.630
I can do this angle; this is a nice angle
for me, but it's a little nostrily.

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:12.670
I think I'm just going to go to the airport.

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:13.980
I know how to make videos there.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:18.370
And here we are, Hank, I gotta tell you, my
boundless enthusiasm for airports, it's a

00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:19.770
little bounded at the moment.

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:22.800
By the way, Hank, if you haven't noticed,
I have completely stopped caring what the

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.180
people in the airport think of me.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:25.900
I live here now; this is my residence.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:29.040
So, Hank, the question I'm asked most often
these days is "Will the Paper Towns movie

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:30.040
be like the book?"

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:32.759
only I don't think they're saying it like
that exactly, I think they're saying it more

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:36.829
like, "Will the Paper Towns movie be exactly
like the book?!", or at least that's how I

00:00:36.829 --> 00:00:37.850
hear it in my head.

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:40.239
The answer is yes, the Paper Towns movie is
like the book.

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:42.499
But also, you know, no it isn't.

00:00:42.499 --> 00:00:45.019
So Hank, when I was a kid, I was really into
novelizations.

00:00:45.019 --> 00:00:47.269
Books that were adapted from TVs or movies
I liked.

00:00:47.269 --> 00:00:49.719
I was particularly into "The Goonies" novelization.

00:00:49.719 --> 00:00:52.639
For those of you who haven't been blessed
by the magic of "The Goonies", it was a movie

00:00:52.639 --> 00:00:56.719
about a bunch of kids who, to save their home,
kind of like discover a pirate ship.

00:00:56.719 --> 00:00:57.719
It's awesome!

00:00:57.719 --> 00:01:00.359
And the thing I love so much about reading
"The Goonies" novelization, was that it was

00:01:00.359 --> 00:01:03.780
like watching the movie in the sense that
all the stuff that happened in the movie,

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:04.780
happened inside my head.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:09.259
But the novelization felt like fresh and exciting
and new in a way that the movie couldn't,

00:01:09.259 --> 00:01:11.670
because I'd already see it like a gajillion
times.

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:15.090
So, because I knew the movie so well, I noticed
all the little changes in the novelization;

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:19.159
like the novel is written from the first person
point of view of one kid and that necessitated

00:01:19.159 --> 00:01:21.770
like some moving around of stuff and some
changes in dialog.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:23.750
And Hank, I loved that!

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:28.149
I loved trying to figure out why the writer
had made changes to the movie in order to

00:01:28.149 --> 00:01:30.420
make the story work as text.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:33.500
Images and sounds just kind of work on us
differently than text does; like one of the

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:37.700
things I love about text-based stories is
that we sort of make them up with the writer.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:42.329
Because the images that are described in the
text only exist inside of my head while I'm

00:01:42.329 --> 00:01:43.329
reading.

00:01:43.329 --> 00:01:46.520
Like Hank, my Harry Potter is different from
your Harry Potter, and every other Harry Potter

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:47.520
on the planet.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.219
My Harry Potter is just mine.

00:01:49.219 --> 00:01:53.759
But anyway, what I really want from an adaptation
is to feel the feelings I felt while reading

00:01:53.759 --> 00:01:54.759
the book, right?

00:01:54.759 --> 00:01:57.509
And personally, I'm willing to sacrifice a
lot of plot points to make that happen, like

00:01:57.509 --> 00:02:01.689
when I wrote my own screenplay of "Paper Towns"
many years ago, it was vastly different from

00:02:01.689 --> 00:02:02.689
the book.

00:02:02.689 --> 00:02:04.950
Like Q ends up chasing after a different girl.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:07.280
Also, there was an airplane crash; it was
just bananas.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:11.360
Anyway, all I wanted was to find a way to
visually tell the story that when you put

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:16.320
people on a pedestal, when you fail to imagine
them complexly, it's tremendously destructive.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:20.100
Now, it turns out that relentlessly focusing
on theme instead of story telling is a bad

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:23.120
strategy which is why that screenplay was
so horrible, but the "Paper Towns" script

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:26.190
that Mike Weber and Scott Neustadter wrote,
the same guys who wrote "The Fault in Our

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:30.290
Stars" screenplay, is just brilliant because
it finds a way to capture both the story and

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:31.290
the ideas.

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:34.410
At least for me, when I watched it, the story
created the same feelings and the characters

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:38.370
had the same relationships with each other,
and the same failures to imagine each other,

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:40.210
and also Santa, complexly.

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:42.950
That said, Hank, if you look for differences,
you'll definitely find lots of them.

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:46.060
Like, for instance, there's no Sea World in
the movie because no one was anxious to pay

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:48.430
Sea World for the chance to give them good
publicity.

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:51.740
Instead, the stuff that happens at Sea World
in the book happens elsewhere in the movie.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:55.500
Also, there's significantly less textual analysis
of Walt Whitman in the movie, which I'm sure

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:56.860
will be disappointing to many.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:57.920
Don't worry, there's still some, though.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:00.120
Also, there's some changes that I think are
just better.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:03.080
Like in the movie, the character Angela joins
in on a road trip that she isn't part of in

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:04.080
the book.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:05.080
And I like that decision.

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:06.090
I wish I'd made it eight years ago.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:08.760
And that's another thing I've learned from
"The Goonies" novelization, Hank, changes

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:11.040
are not necessarily for the worse.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:14.580
In the end, I'd rather have "Paper Towns"
be good than be faithful.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:16.720
But I genuinely believe it is both.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:18.540
So I'm just very grateful to the people who
made it.

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:19.540
I feel very lucky.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:21.490
Hank, your tour is done, congratulations!

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:24.340
I hope you sleep for like three straight days.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:26.950
And then I hope you wake up, because you have
to make a video on Friday.

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:27.440
I'll see you then.

